WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.169
-Our next guest is an
Academy-Award nominated actress

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.504
you know from films such as
"Boys Don't Cry"

00:00:05.504 --> 00:00:07.206
and "Queen and Slim"
and shows like

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:08.908
"Big Love"
and "American Horror Story".

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:11.611
She is starring in the second
isn't of Netflix's

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:13.812
"Russian Doll" and "The Girl
from Plainville,"

00:00:13.812 --> 00:00:15.448
which is available
now on Hulu.

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:16.683
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:20.653
-I have a great mom,
a great dad.

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:22.755
For the most part.

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:25.458
Great parents.

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:28.427
I have all my grandparents.

00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:32.599
But I'm still depressed,

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:34.967
and one of them
is gonna pass away.

00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:38.404
I don't know when, but...

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:42.241
when they do, I'm going to have
to be there for my parents,

00:00:42.241 --> 00:00:44.944
to be there for my mom and dad

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:47.046
whenever their parents
pass away.

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:49.816
-Please welcome to the show,
Chloe Sevigny.

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:52.018
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:52.018 --> 00:01:00.560 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.063 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:05.632
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:07.466
-Thank you.
I'm pleased to be here.

00:01:07.466 --> 00:01:09.536
-This has been sort of a theme
for us this week,

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:12.238
but you also are one of these
people who were lucky enough

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:13.873
to have a pandemic baby.

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:15.408
-I do.
I have a pandemic baby, yes.

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:16.642
-Congratulations.

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:18.310
-Did you get him one of those
t-shirts, like,

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:19.879
"the pandemic ruined
my birthday?"

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:23.716
-Oh, no, but is he about
to turn two?

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:24.784
-He's about to turn two.

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:25.785
We're going to have his
christening.

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:26.819
-Oh, christening, okay.

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:28.521
-Because May is jam-packed
for us.

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:29.655
-Yeah.
-I'm getting married

00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:31.357
at the end of May.
-Oh, congratulations.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:35.261
-So I have, you know, his
birthday, bachelorette, wedding.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:37.597
I was like, how many things can
I invite my friends to?

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:39.565
How many things will they
actually come out for us?

00:01:39.565 --> 00:01:41.868
-That's true.
You got to kind of consolidate.

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:44.136
-Yes.
-Is he old enough --

00:01:44.136 --> 00:01:46.439
is aware that he sort of maybe
missed some more parties than

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:48.774
he would have had at this point?

00:01:48.774 --> 00:01:50.042
-Not yet, thank goodness, no.

00:01:50.042 --> 00:01:52.878
Yes, I'm more like, you know,
everyone is like,

00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:54.114
"Can you see that I'm smiling?"

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:56.048
He doesn't know anything
else but masks.

00:01:56.048 --> 00:01:57.416
Masks have been his whole life.

00:01:57.416 --> 00:01:58.551
-It's going to be amazing.

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:00.520
I think that people, you know,
obviously,

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.856
we're not gonna know for years
how it's actually affected kids,

00:02:02.856 --> 00:02:04.123
but I think it's
going to be so exciting

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:05.891
when they're like, "Wait, people
have had this lower

00:02:05.891 --> 00:02:07.960
half of their face
the whole time?"

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.462
Now, I associate you so much
with New York City,

00:02:10.462 --> 00:02:13.866
and you've lived here
for over two decades, but yet,

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:15.067
you did not grow up
in the city,

00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:16.835
and now you're going to have
a New York kid.

00:02:16.835 --> 00:02:18.938
This is what I think about all
the time, having New York kids.

00:02:18.938 --> 00:02:20.873
How do you feel about it so far?

00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:24.310
What is it you want them to
discover about the city?

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.612
-I just love the neighborhoods
in the city.

00:02:26.612 --> 00:02:28.147
And people always come
to New York

00:02:28.147 --> 00:02:29.648
and they stay in Times Square,
which is fine.

00:02:29.648 --> 00:02:31.917
But I'm like, you should go
and stay in little pockets.

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:33.152
-Yeah.
-And like, discover those

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:34.553
pockets and what's around.

00:02:34.553 --> 00:02:36.688
Like, we go to local
bakery every day,

00:02:36.688 --> 00:02:38.624
the local juice place.

00:02:38.624 --> 00:02:40.559
Pepe Rosso is his favorite
restaurant.

00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:42.395
He's like, "Pepe, Pepe, Pepe."

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:44.163
All he wants to do
is go to Pepe.

00:02:44.163 --> 00:02:46.199
Everybody knows him there
and they pick him up.

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:48.434
And like, yes, we let them
pick him up.

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.203
-Yeah.
Perhaps we shouldn't.

00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:52.972
Maybe we're not the best
parents, but...

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:54.440
-I think if you go to a place
like Pepe Rosso,

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.275
the expectation is someone's
going to pick up your kid.

00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:58.611
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:00.747
Have you had any of those --
you know, again,

00:03:00.747 --> 00:03:02.882
because New York can also be,
I think just

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.350
parenting in general
can be stressful.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.385
Have you had any of the moments
where you bring your kid out

00:03:06.385 --> 00:03:09.955
in the world and you almost
hit a breaking point?

00:03:09.955 --> 00:03:12.057
-Yes, actually, one of the first
times we brought him out,

00:03:12.057 --> 00:03:14.527
I brought him to our local
playground, Vesuvio,

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:18.497
and there was a little
kid there who I could tell,

00:03:18.497 --> 00:03:20.833
like, I already had a little bit
of a problem with this kid.

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:22.568
-Yeah.
-I was not so into this kid.

00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:23.970
He was, like, maybe three,

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.337
and I was like,
this kid's got attitude.

00:03:25.337 --> 00:03:27.072
There is something
about this kid.

00:03:27.072 --> 00:03:29.842
And I had Vanja, my son,
on my lap like this,

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:31.577
and the kid throws
something at us.

00:03:31.577 --> 00:03:34.179
It was like, this part
of a lock, like, a metal thing.

00:03:34.179 --> 00:03:35.548
I was like [ Gasps ].

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:37.550
I didn't know
what I was going to do.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.018
I was like,
do I yell at the kid?

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:40.853
Do I yell at the mom?
Do I yell at both of them?

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:43.222
I was losing my mind.

00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:46.058
So I had to get up, walk out,
calm down, breathe,

00:03:46.058 --> 00:03:48.094
and like call my sister-in-law
like, "What do I do?"

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:49.194
She was like, "you're gonna have
to see this woman

00:03:49.194 --> 00:03:50.630
all the time at the playground.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:53.066
You better not pick
a fight with her."

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:54.233
And I was like,
"What about the kid?

00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:56.569
Can I pick a fight
with the kid?"

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:58.671
And now I see this kid walking
around on Prince Street.

00:03:58.671 --> 00:03:59.939
And I'm like, this kid.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:01.507
I got it out for this kid.

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:04.177
-Yeah. So, your son is over it,
but you're not?

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:05.844
-No.
-Every time you pass him,

00:04:05.844 --> 00:04:08.080
you're like, "I know you.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:10.216
I remember what you did."

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:12.084
So you have two amazing
shows out right now.

00:04:12.084 --> 00:04:13.585
I want to talk about "Girl from
Plainville" real quick.

00:04:13.585 --> 00:04:15.921
You're getting incredible
reviews, well deserved.

00:04:15.921 --> 00:04:17.757
We're watching the show,
my wife and I.

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:21.126
It's very intense.

00:04:21.126 --> 00:04:22.962
Based on a true story.

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:25.931
Was it an exciting role
to take on?

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:28.367
Because obviously, there is a
lot behind it.

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:30.502
-I mean, "exciting," I'm not
sure that's the right word.

00:04:30.502 --> 00:04:34.406
But it's challenging,
and I watched the documentary

00:04:34.406 --> 00:04:36.408
that our show is kind of based
on, "I Love You, Now Die."

00:04:36.408 --> 00:04:39.678
I was very struck by the woman
they asked me to play.

00:04:39.678 --> 00:04:42.080
Just her strength of character,
her resolve,

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.450
how soulful she was and how she
spoke about her son,

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.685
and how she really wanted people
to hear her son's story,

00:04:46.685 --> 00:04:49.989
and that really propelled me to
get on board with the project.

00:04:49.989 --> 00:04:53.926
And Elle Fanning is in it,
who I love and adore.

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:55.394
-Yeah, she's really
wonderful in it.

00:04:55.394 --> 00:04:56.996
-I think she's got great taste
and she's an amazing actress,

00:04:56.996 --> 00:05:00.199
and I think it's great for all
of her young fans to tune in

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:02.635
and really think about the way
they communicate via text.

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:05.404
It's so easy to read something
and just write

00:05:05.404 --> 00:05:06.973
a flippant response,
but, you know,

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:09.441
we really have to consider.

00:05:09.441 --> 00:05:12.178
-Yeah. You also to to --

00:05:12.178 --> 00:05:14.713
I feel like, less challenging,
certainly, emotionally,

00:05:14.713 --> 00:05:17.116
but you did have to do
a New England accident.

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:18.351
I will say, as a New Englander,

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:19.618
I thought you did
a very nice job.

00:05:19.618 --> 00:05:21.154
-Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:05:21.154 --> 00:05:22.522
I mean, it's one of those
ones that sticks out

00:05:22.522 --> 00:05:24.757
like a sore thumb when it's not.

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:27.026
-I would think I'm pretty bad
at it, but, yes.

00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:28.260
Yeah, I tried. I tried.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.062
There is different levels of it
in the show,

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:33.199
so I think I kind of get away
with my minimal version.

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:34.300
-You did very nice job.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:35.367
This is also very exciting.

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:37.202
"Russian Doll"
with Natasha Lyonne,

00:05:37.202 --> 00:05:39.471
which is a show,
the first season I loved,

00:05:39.471 --> 00:05:40.773
I was not alone in loving.

00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:42.708
You've known Natasha forever.

00:05:42.708 --> 00:05:45.244
Did you jump at the chance
to do season 2 with her?

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:47.647
-Yeah, of course.
I mean, I was in season 1.

00:05:47.647 --> 00:05:52.951
One small, pivotal episode,
and she asked me to come back,

00:05:52.951 --> 00:05:54.653
and I said, of course, I want to
hang out my my friends.

00:05:54.653 --> 00:05:55.955
Who doesn't want to go to work
with their friends?

00:05:55.955 --> 00:05:57.823
You know, you're my best friend.
You're hilarious.

00:05:57.823 --> 00:05:59.158
You're fun to be around.
You're passionate.

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:00.826
I love your -- I love your show.

00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:02.895
There was a lot of pressure
for the sophomoric effort.

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:04.196
-Yes, of course.
-You know, for the second

00:06:04.196 --> 00:06:06.165
season, everybody's like,
is this going to be as good?

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:08.000
So it was very, like,
high stakes.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.502
-And she also directed a lot.

00:06:10.502 --> 00:06:14.440
As a friend, does she direct
well, knowing that you're

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:17.109
also her friend and not just
an actor in her show?

00:06:17.109 --> 00:06:19.145
-Yeah, I mean -- yeah.

00:06:19.145 --> 00:06:21.848
[ Laughter ]

00:06:21.848 --> 00:06:24.783
You know, she can probably get
away with a few more things.

00:06:24.783 --> 00:06:26.919
-Yeah.
-That I let slide.

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:30.056
I want to try and help her relax
into it, enjoy it,

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:32.691
and so maybe some of the
snapping was directed at me,

00:06:32.691 --> 00:06:33.959
because I could take it.

00:06:33.959 --> 00:06:35.060
-Yeah, that's true.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:36.362
At the end of the day,
you guys had

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:38.063
the infrastructure in place
that she could maybe

00:06:38.063 --> 00:06:39.264
be a little bit harder on you.

00:06:39.264 --> 00:06:41.701
-That's right.
Just let it all out on me.

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:43.602
-I'm so excited to see that,

00:06:43.602 --> 00:06:46.005
and congratulations again on
"Girl from Plainville."

00:06:46.005 --> 00:06:47.540
It's really wonderful, and it's
so nice to have you here.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:49.975
-Thank you so much.
-You guys, that's Chloe Sevigny.

00:06:49.975 --> 00:06:52.512
Second season of "Russian Doll"
is streaming now on Netflix,

00:06:52.512 --> 00:06:55.180
and new episodes of "The Girl
from Plainville"

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:56.449
air Tuesdays on Hulu.

